= Solution
{parent=a}

Use the <predictable sigma-algebra> generated by $A\times\{0\}$, $A\in\mathcal F_0$, and $A\times(s,t]$, $A\in\mathcal F_s$. A <previsible process> is a measurable function for this sigma-algebra. Set $J_t=\mathbf1_{\{S<t\leq T\}}$, with $J_0=0$. The events $\{S<t\}$ and $\{T<t\}$ are in $\mathcal F_t$, because they are countable unions of events $\{S\leq q\}$ and $\{T\leq q\}$ for rational $q<t$. Thus $J$ is an <adapted process>. Each path is left-continuous, including at the two endpoints, and is zero if $S=T$.

For a direct measurability argument, let
$$
J_t^{(n)}=\sum_{k\geq0}J_{k2^{-n}}\mathbf1_{(k2^{-n},(k+1)2^{-n}]}(t).
$$
Each summand is measurable for the <predictable sigma-algebra>, since its coefficient is $\mathcal F_{k2^{-n}}$-measurable. At every $t>0$ the sampling time approaches $t$ strictly from below, so left-continuity gives $J_t^{(n)}\to J_t$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbf1_{(S,T]}\text{ is previsible}.}
$$
The original PDF has a right-closed interval. The open right endpoint in the converted TeX is a transcription error; replacing $(S,T]$ by $(S,T)$ is not valid for arbitrary <stopping times>.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

An <elementary predictable process with stopping-time intervals> has the form $H=\sum_{j=0}^{r-1}h_j\mathbf1_{(\tau_j,\tau_{j+1}]}$, where the <stopping times> are ordered and $h_j$ is bounded and $\mathcal F_{\tau_j}$-measurable. Deterministic endpoints give the usual <simple predictable process>. Define the zero-starting <stochastic integral> by
$$
\boxed{(H\cdot M)_t=\sum_{j=0}^{r-1}h_j(M_{t\wedge\tau_{j+1}}-M_{t\wedge\tau_j}).}
$$
Common refinement and telescoping show that this definition is independent of the representation. Use $M_\infty$ for an infinite endpoint. The <L2-bounded martingale convergence theorem> gives convergence of $M_t$ in $L^2$ to $M_\infty$ and $M_t=\mathbb E[M_\infty\mid\mathcal F_t]$.

Put $D_j=M_{\tau_{j+1}}-M_{\tau_j}$. The <optional sampling theorem> gives $\mathbb E[D_j\mid\mathcal F_{\tau_j}]=0$. For $j<k$, the variable $h_jD_jh_k$ is $\mathcal F_{\tau_k}$-measurable; hence $\mathbb E[h_jD_jh_kD_k]=0$. Applying the same theorem to the assumed <martingale> $M^2-[M]$ gives
$$
\mathbb E[D_j^2\mid\mathcal F_{\tau_j}]=\mathbb E[[M]_{\tau_{j+1}}-[M]_{\tau_j}\mid\mathcal F_{\tau_j}].
$$
Consequently the <Itô isometry> follows:
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[(H\cdot M)_\infty^2]=\sum_j\mathbb E[h_j^2([M]_{\tau_{j+1}}-[M]_{\tau_j})]=\mathbb E\int_0^\infty H_s^2\,d[M]_s.}
$$
The unbounded <stopping times> cause no optional-sampling gap. The $L^2$ terminal representation makes $M$ <uniformly integrable> and makes the family of stopped $M^2$ <uniformly integrable>, by conditional Jensen applied to $M_\infty^2$. Also $\mathbb E[M]_\infty=\mathbb E M_\infty^2-\mathbb E M_0^2<\infty$, from <monotone convergence theorem> and the assumed square-compensator identity. Thus $M^2-[M]$ has the needed uniform integrability, and truncating <stopping times> and passing in $L^1$ is legitimate. Bounded coefficients are part of the simple-integrand convention; more general coefficients require the displayed square-integrability condition.

= c
